-- the table begins. Each compound is numbered from the carboxyl carbon or the first carbon, its
-- carbon-bound hydrogens are listed, and its symmetries are given as cycles and exchanges. The two
-- counts are computed and the observed count is stated in the comment from the published spectrum. A
-- compound where the two counts agree is as much a part of the table as one where they differ: a
-- construction that predicted splitting everywhere would be no better than one that predicted it
-- nowhere.
--
-- alanine, the first agreement case. Atoms: 0 carboxyl carbon, 1 and 2 its oxygens, 3 the alpha carbon,
-- 4 its hydrogen, 5 the nitrogen, 6 the methyl carbon, 7 to 9 its hydrogens. The methyl rotation is a
-- proper operation and the only symmetry there is, so both readings give the same classes.
num-ala-obs : List Nat
num-ala-obs = cons 4 (cons 7 (cons 8 (cons 9 nil)))
